Types of Caching


1. Browser Cache
Stores website resources like images, CSS, and JavaScript inside the user’s browser.

Example: Chrome stores website images locally to load pages faster.

2. Memory Cache (In-Memory Cache)
Stores data in RAM for extremely fast access.

Example Technologies:

Redis
Memcached

3. Database Cache
Caches frequently executed database queries and records.

Benefit: Reduces database read operations.

4. Application Cache
Stores application-level data like configurations, sessions, and computed results.

Used In: Backend services and APIs.

5. CDN Cache
Caches static content on edge servers closer to users.

Examples:

Cloudflare
Akamai Technologies

6. DNS Cache
Stores domain name lookups temporarily.

Benefit: Speeds up website access by avoiding repeated DNS resolution.

7. API Cache
Stores API responses for repeated requests.

Common Use Case: Weather APIs, payment APIs, AI APIs.

8. Disk Cache
Stores cached data on storage devices instead of RAM.

Benefit: Useful for large datasets.

9. Distributed Cache
Cache shared across multiple servers or services.

Used In: Large-scale microservices architecture.

10. Session Cache
Stores user session and authentication data.

Example: Login session tokens.

11. Object Cache
Caches complete objects or serialized data.

Common In: ORM frameworks and web applications.

12. Query Cache
Caches SQL query results.

Benefit: Avoids executing the same query repeatedly.

13. Page Cache
Stores fully rendered web pages.

Common In: WordPress and CMS platforms.

14. Opcode Cache
Caches compiled code instead of recompiling every request.

Example: PHP OPcache.

15. Reverse Proxy Cache
Caches responses between client and backend server.

Examples:

Nginx
Varnish Cache

16. Edge Cache
Stores data at edge locations geographically near users.

Benefit: Reduces latency globally.

17. CPU Cache
Hardware-level cache inside processors.

Types: L1, L2, L3 Cache.

18. GPU Cache
Used in graphics processing and AI workloads.

Benefit: Improves rendering and parallel computations.

19. AI/LLM Cache
Caches prompts, embeddings, and generated responses.

Used In: Chatbots and AI assistants.

20. Write-Through / Write-Back Cache
Caching strategies used in storage and databases.

Write-Through: Updates cache and database together.
Write-Back: Updates cache first and database later.

