🗂️ Caching Strategies 


Cache-aside



Cache-Aside is also called Lazy Loading
Application manages both cache and database
Redis is commonly used as cache
DynamoDB/MySQL/PostgreSQL act as primary database
Database is the source of truth

Step-by-Step Flow
User sends request
Application checks Redis cache
If Cache Hit → return cached data
If Cache Miss → fetch from database
Store database result into Redis
Return response to user

Read Flow
Check cache first
Cache hit gives very fast response
Cache miss triggers DB query
Retrieved data gets cached
Future requests become faster

Write Flow
Update data in database
Delete old cache entry
Next read fetches fresh data
Fresh data is stored back in cache

Why Cache is Deleted After Write
Prevent stale data
Ensure latest DB data is served
Force cache refresh on next request

Cache Hit
Data found in Redis
Very low latency
Fast API response
Reduces DB load

Cache Miss
Data not available in cache
Request goes to database
Slightly slower response
Cache gets updated afterward

Advantages
Faster read performance
Lower database traffic
Better scalability
Reduced infrastructure cost
Easy to implement
Efficient memory usage

Disadvantages
Cache invalidation complexity
Risk of stale data
First request latency
Cache stampede issues
Extra cache management needed

Best Practices
Use TTL (Time To Live)
Invalidate cache after updates
Cache only frequently used data
Monitor cache hit ratio
Use distributed locking for hot keys

