Write-through

Write-Through Cache Pattern
Write-Through is a synchronous caching strategy
Data is written to cache and database together
Cache always stays updated with latest data
Commonly used when consistency is important
Redis acts as cache layer
DynamoDB/MySQL/PostgreSQL act as primary database

Step-by-Step Flow
User sends write request
Application writes data to Redis cache
Same data is immediately written to database
Both cache and database stay synchronized
Success response returned to user

Read Flow
Application checks cache first
Cache usually contains latest data
Data returned directly from Redis
Database reads are reduced

Write Flow
Write request received
Update cache immediately
Update database immediately
Return success only after both succeed

Main Goal
Keep cache and database consistent
Avoid stale cache data
Ensure fresh reads from cache

Advantages
Cache always has latest data
Strong consistency
Faster read operations
Simplifies cache management
No stale cache problem
Good for critical systems

Disadvantages
Write operations become slower
Higher write latency
Every write updates two systems
Unused data may still occupy cache memory
Increased infrastructure cost

Best Use Cases
Banking systems
Payment applications
Inventory management
Order management systems
Financial applications
Real-time transactional systems

Cache Hit
Data found in Redis
Very fast response
Latest data usually available

Cache Miss
Rare in write-through systems
Cache already populated during writes

Best Practices
Use durable cache storage
Monitor write latency
Use retry mechanisms
Handle partial failures carefully
Use replication for high availability

